{"qid":"Q1311219","names":["Jaffna kingdom"],"title":"Jaffna kingdom","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Jaffna kingdom (also known as the Kingdom of Aryachakravarti) was a Tamil polity in the northern tip of Sri Lanka, emerging from the ruins of Kalinga Magha's Polonnaruwa-based conquest around 1215 CE and consolidating under the Aryachakravarti dynasty, with Nallur as its capital. At its height in the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries the kingdom controlled the Jaffna Peninsula, the northern littoral, and exacted tribute from the Vanni chieftaincies of the interior, engaging in extensive maritime trade with South India and exporting horses, elephants, and cloth. The kingdom came under increasing pressure from the Kingdom of Kotte in the south from the mid-fifteenth century and fell definitively to Portuguese forces in 1619 under Captain-General Constantino de Sá de Noronha, who captured and executed the last king, Cankili II, ending Tamil political independence in Sri Lanka for over three centuries.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The territorial extent of the Jaffna kingdom is moderately attested from Tamil inscriptions, Portuguese colonial records, and Sinhala chronicles, though the precise control over the Vanni interior fluctuated significantly over its four centuries.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Jaffna kingdom","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jaffna_kingdom"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Aryachakravarti dynasty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aryachakravarti_dynasty"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Cankili II","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cankili_II"}],"britannica":"place/Jaffna-